---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<Layout title="Philosophy | Goobus' Personal Site">
  <p class="terminal-prompt">
    ls ~/philosophy
    <span class="cursor" aria-hidden="true">
      _
    </span>
  </p>

  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>My Philosophy</h1>
    <p class="description">
      <i>
        My thoughts on the relationship between coding, math, and philosophy as 
        a 14 y.o. kid. I try to answer why I code, with a bit of philosophy. 
        Take what you need, leave what you don't.
      </i>
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="content">
    <p>
      I recently came across a Youtube channel named <a 
      href="https://www.youtube.com/@hayyoth-org">Hayyoth ➚</a>. His programming
      style is different from almost everyone else that I've seen - extreme minimalism.
      No AI, no CSS, no autocomplete, not even auto-brace-closing (typing opening brace
      doesn't automatically insert a closing brace). He does use light theme though, but 
      I'm not here to judge. His programming skills made up for it. Some called him the 
      "Asian Tsoding," or something like that. 
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uRLMMYFoGB4">This video ➚</a> of his explains
      why he chose extreme minimalism. People are losing their ability to code without a 
      ChatGPT tab open on their 2nd monitor. He took an extreme path to prove a point - 
      that raw human skill beats AI vibe-coded slop. Something about that concept really 
      made me realize, that although I claim the title of "Anti-Vibe Coding," I may be 
      becoming the very thing that I am against. 
    </p>

    <p>
      From the time I'm writing this, I use AI to explain design concepts, language philosophies, and 
      best practices. I use AI to help correct mistakes in READMEs and devlogs (my English is bad, 
      don't judge I'm only 14. Also this article was fully written without AI). But never once did 
      I ask an AI to implement a function or style an element.
    </p>

    <p>
      I became intrigued by Hayyoth and his radical commitment. His website was something 
      to be considered "poorly" styled. But why does spending 12 hours on colouring boxes 
      and smoothing edges matter? I'm a C++ developer, and its good practice to enclose
      all your project code with a project-global namespace. If it was me 2 months ago, I 
      would have done exactly that. But I've never written a
      C++ library before, not even in any language.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now, like I mentioned, I am only 14. Take what I'm about to say with a grain of salt.
      Philosophy is far more complicated than my preception of it, and I am fully aware.
      Hayyoth, in his <a href="https://hayyoth.org/white-paper">White Paper ➚</a>, seems to 
      discuss the relations between Math and Science to Philosphy. A concept he probably 
      holds is Mathematical Platonism. It is the idea that mathematical entities like numbers,
      sets, shapes, laws, and formulas exist <i>independently</i> of the human mind.
      If one day, the sun explodes and all humans go extinct, all of these abstract concepts
      would still be held in place.
    </p>

    <p>
      A criticism of this view would be the Epistemological Problem. The rebuttal goes something 
      like this: if the abstract mathematical concepts exist completely seperate from space, time,
      and physical causes, how can human beings interact with them? After all, if human beings
      are just purely atoms formed by chance, and our consciousness is nothing but physical 
      reactions, then surely we can't interact with something that is beyond the physical world.
    </p>

    <p>
      My response to this criticism stems from my personal beliefs. I'm not saying that my 
      worldview is the only one that is compatible with Mathematical Platonism, nor is this
      an attempt at proving it. I am too much of a pathetic crumb at subjects like theology, 
      metaphysics, and philosophy of religion to try (I do watch <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trent_Horn">
      Trent Horn ➚</a>, you should too regardless of personal beliefs). My rebuttal would 
      introduce on top of Math Platonism; Divine Conceptualism.
    </p>

    <p>
      My response is that, said subjects are not laws above God, nor were created by God. Rather, they are aspects
      reflecting His nature. And since God cannot contradict Himself, that is what makes some
      things objective. I once heard an athiest argue that because morality is what God decides
      is right, that makes morality subjective, since God is a subject. I would argue that 
      God is immutable, unchanging, and a metaphysical being, to assume that He is a subject 
      like the rest of us would be incorrect. And because morality is in His nature, it is 
      equally objective, immutable, and eternal as He is. And I would argue that math is also
      in His nature - objective, immutable, eternal, and independent from all thinking rational 
      beings.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Bible says that God created humans in his image. We were created with connection to 
      him. That is why we all have some sort of morality, no matter how messed up you are. That 
      is why we all have intrinsic need for something greater than ourselves. This is why we 
      seek for something more - humans invented gods and deities all throughout history. They
      sacrifice babies to please their own creation. Man is a religious creature - we have 
      partial knowledge of God's nature. We just can't fully grasp it, hence why we make 
      gods, deities, and man-made religions. In the same way that we have partial knowledge of 
      one aspect of God's nature, we also have partial knowledge of things like math, science,
      and logic.
    </p>

    <p>
      Here is my line of reasoning.  
      <br>
      1. God is rational and the source of objective truth. <br>
      2. Humans are created in God's image.  <br>
      3. Human rationality is therefore a reflection, however imperfect, of divine rationality.  <br>
      4. Mathematical reasoning allows us to comprehend truths that aren't dependent on physical observations
    </p>

    <p>
      That really makes me think; we barely know anything about theology - yet so much about
      math, science and physics. Maybe we actually <strong>don't</strong> know as much as 
      we thought we did. With the recent advancement in quantum mechanics, it really goes to show
      how much we think we know vs how much we really know. Religions claim to know so 
      much about the divine, yet so much of that is wrong or incomplete. Doctrines are 
      changed over time, just like math, science, philosophy, and virtually any other fields.
      Maybe we are just foolish little humans, claiming to know what we don't.
    </p>

    <p>
      Why exactly do I code? Well, I think math, computer science, physics, chemistry, any 
      scientific fields, any school of philosophy, anything abstract that we can't see with our 
      eyes but know its there, they all reflect God's nature. In John 3:8, we see that God is 
      like the wind; we can't see it, but we know its there. Now, wind is definitely more 
      percievable than existential questions, like "why are we here?" or "what is our purpose?"
      But, we realize that this was written to an audience of ancient peasants. The average 
      Joe doesn't understand shit about, say, the cardinality of integers or quantum 
      superpositions (I don't either :D). We need to adapt to scripture; timeless wisdom 
      is timeless of a reason.
    </p>

    <p>
      I think anything and everything "nerdy" all have deeper connections to the divine, as 
      I stated in the previous paragraphs, how if God really does exist, everything would
      point to him. Now, I could lie about "I code for the greater good" or something, but 
      truth is, I really don't. I code for fun. But heres the duality of things - we live 
      because we are brought here and we can taste, see, and enjoy the things in our lives.
      But just because we have those reasons to live, doesn't mean we shouldn't search 
      for deeper truths.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thanks for reading all that (if you didn't just scroll all the way to the bottom). Please do 
      note, that I am not taking philosophy in school or anything. I don't know a lot of what I 
      talk about. 
    </p>

    <p>
      In conclusion, what I wrote centered around "recognizing the existience of something
      we cannot fully understand, and pursuing that thing." 
    </p>

    <p>
      We can predict mathematical outcomes without
      necesarrily understanding why it is so effective at describing reality. 
    </p>

    <p>
      We can use quantum mechanics to make accurate predictions while still disagreeing about 
      its nature and why it really matter.
    </p>

    <p>
      And, we can push all that mess aside, and say to 
      ourselves, fuck it, let us code something cool today. 
      Let us explore things we don't understand. 
      Let us walk blindly in a dark room, feeling
      around to not bump into things. Let us shine a flashlight into 
      the abyss and pretend 
      it does something. Maybe that is the only way we can understand what is <u>beyond</u>.
    </p>
  </div>

  
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-header {
    margin: 0rem auto 2.5rem;
    max-width: 70vw;
  }

  .page-header h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
  }

  .description {
    max-width: 42rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .content p {
    width: 70vw;
    margin: 1.5rem auto 1.5rem;
  }

  a {
    color: var(--color-bright);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  a:hover {
    color: var(--color-less-bright)
  }
</style>


